VMA -- Virtual Memory Areas
===========================

Per-process record of *what* memory ranges are mapped. The page
table records *what is currently resident*; the VMA list records
*what should be there*. The two diverge under demand paging — a
VMA exists for a range, but the PTE is non-present until first
access. The page-fault handler consults the VMA list to decide
whether to install a fresh frame, copy-on-write, page-in from a
file, or kill the process.

Master-roadmap invariant 1: per-process VMA list, not fixed
regions. This module is the foundation chunk 1 lays before Phase
1's demand-paging / fork / exec work hits.

VMAs in a process never overlap. They form a sorted, disjoint
cover of the user portion of the address space. Lookups are by
address (point query): "which VMA covers `vaddr`?". This is
served by a red-black tree keyed on `start`, which gives O(log
n) insert, remove, and predecessor walk. (Augmented interval
trees aren't needed since VMAs don't overlap — the predecessor
of `vaddr` is the only candidate to check `vaddr < end` against.)

The pool is process-shared (one global free list). Each process
holds a `VMATree` root index. `vma_*` operations take the tree
by `*VMATree` and the global pool is module state.

This chunk is data-structure-only. No kernel codepath consults
the VMA list yet — the existing fixed-region eager-mapping in
`vm_create_process_pt` is unchanged. Chunk 2 wires the page-
fault handler through `vma_lookup_addr`; chunk 3 makes
`vm_create_process_pt` lazy.

    module oskit.kernel

    import oskit.config.{MAX_VMAS, PAGE_SIZE}
    import oskit.arch.{vm_install_user_page, vm_update_user_prot, vm_unmap_user_page, vm_v2p}

VMA_NIL serves the same role as rbtree.lsysl's NIL — a sentinel
"no node" value distinct from any pool index.

    val VMA_NIL = -1

POSIX-style protection bits, used as the `prot` field of a VMA.
These match the values userspace uses for `mmap`/`mprotect`.

    val VMA_PROT_NONE  = 0
    val VMA_PROT_READ  = 1
    val VMA_PROT_WRITE = 2
    val VMA_PROT_EXEC  = 4

POSIX-style mapping bits, used as the low half of `flags`. The
high half is reserved for VMA-internal flags (COW, growsdown,
etc.) so an `mmap`-style argument can be stored without conflict.

    val VMA_MAP_SHARED  = 0x01
    val VMA_MAP_PRIVATE = 0x02
    val VMA_MAP_FIXED   = 0x10
    val VMA_MAP_ANON    = 0x20

Internal bits — set by the kernel, never accepted from userspace.

    val VMA_FLAG_COW       = 0x10000
    val VMA_FLAG_GROWSDOWN = 0x20000

Red-black colors. RED on insert, BLACK after fixup balances. The
root is always BLACK.

    val VMA_RB_BLACK = 0
    val VMA_RB_RED   = 1

A VMA carries the user-visible mapping fields (start/end/prot/
flags/fd/offset) plus its rb-tree-node fields (left/right/parent/
color) and a free-list link. Inlining the tree node into the VMA
struct avoids a parallel array — the same pool index serves both
the data and the tree node.

    struct VMA
        vstart: u64
        vend:   u64
        prot:   int
        flags:  int
        fd:     int
        offset: u64
        left:   int
        right:  int
        parent: int
        color:  int
        next_free: int

A `VMATree` is just the root index. Each process owns one. The
struct exists (instead of a bare `int` field) so operations can
take `*VMATree` and update `root` via field assignment, which
matches the rbtree.lsysl convention.

    struct VMATree
        root: int

Module-level pool. `MAX_VMAS` is set in `sysl.toml`/`oskit.config`
so the limit is visible everywhere it matters. Static array; no
allocator dependency.

    var vma_pool: [4096]VMA
    var vma_free_head: int = 0
    var vma_pool_init = false

`vma_module_init` is idempotent and called lazily by `vma_alloc`.
The first allocation links every slot into the free list. Doing
it lazily keeps `kernel_init` free of an explicit init call.

    vma_module_init()
        if vma_pool_init
            return
        var i = 0
        while i < MAX_VMAS - 1
            vma_pool[i].next_free = i + 1
            i += 1
        vma_pool[MAX_VMAS - 1].next_free = VMA_NIL
        vma_free_head = 0
        vma_pool_init = true

Take a VMA from the free list and zero its tree-node fields. The
caller fills in start/end/prot/flags/fd/offset before inserting.

    vma_alloc() -> int
        vma_module_init()
        val idx = vma_free_head
        if idx == VMA_NIL
            return VMA_NIL
        vma_free_head = vma_pool[idx].next_free
        vma_pool[idx].left   = VMA_NIL
        vma_pool[idx].right  = VMA_NIL
        vma_pool[idx].parent = VMA_NIL
        vma_pool[idx].color  = VMA_RB_RED
        vma_pool[idx].next_free = VMA_NIL
        idx

Return a VMA to the free list. Caller is responsible for having
already removed it from any tree.

    vma_free(idx: int)
        if idx == VMA_NIL
            return
        vma_pool[idx].next_free = vma_free_head
        vma_free_head = idx

Initialize a freshly-allocated `VMATree` to "empty". Always called
from process creation.

    vma_tree_init(t: *VMATree)
        t.root = VMA_NIL

Color helpers — NIL is treated as BLACK.

    vma_color(idx: int) -> int
        if idx == VMA_NIL
            return VMA_RB_BLACK
        vma_pool[idx].color

    vma_set_color(idx: int, c: int)
        if idx != VMA_NIL
            vma_pool[idx].color = c

Standard BST rotations. `rotate_left` at `x` makes `x.right`
the new subtree root.

    vma_rotate_left(t: *VMATree, x: int)
        val y = vma_pool[x].right
        vma_pool[x].right = vma_pool[y].left
        if vma_pool[y].left != VMA_NIL
            vma_pool[vma_pool[y].left].parent = x
        vma_pool[y].parent = vma_pool[x].parent
        if vma_pool[x].parent == VMA_NIL
            t.root = y
        else
            if x == vma_pool[vma_pool[x].parent].left
                vma_pool[vma_pool[x].parent].left = y
            else
                vma_pool[vma_pool[x].parent].right = y
        vma_pool[y].left = x
        vma_pool[x].parent = y

    vma_rotate_right(t: *VMATree, y: int)
        val x = vma_pool[y].left
        vma_pool[y].left = vma_pool[x].right
        if vma_pool[x].right != VMA_NIL
            vma_pool[vma_pool[x].right].parent = y
        vma_pool[x].parent = vma_pool[y].parent
        if vma_pool[y].parent == VMA_NIL
            t.root = x
        else
            if y == vma_pool[vma_pool[y].parent].left
                vma_pool[vma_pool[y].parent].left = x
            else
                vma_pool[vma_pool[y].parent].right = x
        vma_pool[x].right = y
        vma_pool[y].parent = x

Restore red-black invariants after insertion. `z` is the freshly-
inserted (red) node. Walk up fixing red-red violations by
recoloring or rotating; the root is forced BLACK at the end.

    vma_insert_fixup(t: *VMATree, z_in: int)
        var z = z_in
        while vma_pool[z].parent != VMA_NIL
            val p = vma_pool[z].parent
            if vma_pool[p].color != VMA_RB_RED
                break
            val gp = vma_pool[p].parent
            if gp == VMA_NIL
                break
            if p == vma_pool[gp].left
                val uncle = vma_pool[gp].right
                if vma_color(uncle) == VMA_RB_RED
                    vma_pool[p].color = VMA_RB_BLACK
                    vma_set_color(uncle, VMA_RB_BLACK)
                    vma_pool[gp].color = VMA_RB_RED
                    z = gp
                else
                    if z == vma_pool[p].right
                        z = p
                        vma_rotate_left(t, z)
                    val p2 = vma_pool[z].parent
                    val gp2 = vma_pool[p2].parent
                    vma_pool[p2].color = VMA_RB_BLACK
                    vma_pool[gp2].color = VMA_RB_RED
                    vma_rotate_right(t, gp2)
            else
                val uncle = vma_pool[gp].left
                if vma_color(uncle) == VMA_RB_RED
                    vma_pool[p].color = VMA_RB_BLACK
                    vma_set_color(uncle, VMA_RB_BLACK)
                    vma_pool[gp].color = VMA_RB_RED
                    z = gp
                else
                    if z == vma_pool[p].left
                        z = p
                        vma_rotate_right(t, z)
                    val p2 = vma_pool[z].parent
                    val gp2 = vma_pool[p2].parent
                    vma_pool[p2].color = VMA_RB_BLACK
                    vma_pool[gp2].color = VMA_RB_RED
                    vma_rotate_left(t, gp2)
        vma_pool[t.root].color = VMA_RB_BLACK

Insert a fresh VMA into the tree, keyed on `start`. Caller has
already filled in start/end/prot/flags/fd/offset on the freshly
allocated index. Returns the inserted node index (same as `idx`)
for convenience. Does *not* check for overlap — callers are
expected to use `vma_lookup_addr` first when overlap matters.

    vma_insert(t: *VMATree, idx: int)
        var parent = VMA_NIL
        var cur = t.root
        val key = vma_pool[idx].vstart
        while cur != VMA_NIL
            parent = cur
            if key < vma_pool[cur].vstart
                cur = vma_pool[cur].left
            else
                cur = vma_pool[cur].right
        vma_pool[idx].parent = parent
        if parent == VMA_NIL
            t.root = idx
        else
            if key < vma_pool[parent].vstart
                vma_pool[parent].left = idx
            else
                vma_pool[parent].right = idx
        vma_insert_fixup(t, idx)

Convenience wrapper: allocate a VMA, fill it in, insert it,
return the index. Returns `VMA_NIL` if the pool is exhausted.

    vma_create(t: *VMATree, vstart: u64, vend: u64, prot: int, flags: int, fd: int, offset: u64) -> int
        val idx = vma_alloc()
        if idx == VMA_NIL
            return VMA_NIL
        vma_pool[idx].vstart  = vstart
        vma_pool[idx].vend    = vend
        vma_pool[idx].prot   = prot
        vma_pool[idx].flags  = flags
        vma_pool[idx].fd     = fd
        vma_pool[idx].offset = offset
        vma_insert(t, idx)
        idx

Point query: which VMA covers `addr`? Returns the VMA index, or
`VMA_NIL` if no VMA covers `addr`.

The query exploits the disjoint-VMA invariant: walk down the BST
tracking the largest node whose start ≤ addr; that is the only
candidate, and it covers `addr` iff `addr < end`.

    vma_lookup_addr(t: *VMATree, addr: u64) -> int
        var cur = t.root
        var best = VMA_NIL
        while cur != VMA_NIL
            if vma_pool[cur].vstart <= addr
                best = cur
                cur = vma_pool[cur].right
            else
                cur = vma_pool[cur].left
        if best == VMA_NIL
            return VMA_NIL
        if addr < vma_pool[best].vend
            return best
        VMA_NIL

In-order successor / predecessor — used by `vma_remove` and by
range walks (chunks 4-6). NIL if none.

    vma_min(idx_in: int) -> int
        if idx_in == VMA_NIL
            return VMA_NIL
        var cur = idx_in
        while vma_pool[cur].left != VMA_NIL
            cur = vma_pool[cur].left
        cur

    vma_first(t: *VMATree) -> int
        vma_min(t.root)

    vma_next(idx: int) -> int
        if idx == VMA_NIL
            return VMA_NIL
        if vma_pool[idx].right != VMA_NIL
            return vma_min(vma_pool[idx].right)
        var cur = idx
        var p = vma_pool[cur].parent
        while p != VMA_NIL
            if vma_pool[p].left == cur
                return p
            cur = p
            p = vma_pool[cur].parent
        VMA_NIL

Replace one subtree by another. `transplant(u, v)` swaps `v` into
`u`'s position in the tree; `u` becomes detached.

    vma_transplant(t: *VMATree, u: int, v: int)
        if vma_pool[u].parent == VMA_NIL
            t.root = v
        else
            val p = vma_pool[u].parent
            if u == vma_pool[p].left
                vma_pool[p].left = v
            else
                vma_pool[p].right = v
        if v != VMA_NIL
            vma_pool[v].parent = vma_pool[u].parent

Restore red-black invariants after removal. `x` is the node that
took the place of the removed black node (possibly NIL); `xp` is
its parent (tracked separately because `x` may be NIL and have no
parent pointer of its own).

    vma_remove_fixup(t: *VMATree, x_in: int, xp_in: int)
        var x = x_in
        var xp = xp_in
        while x != t.root
            if vma_color(x) == VMA_RB_RED
                break
            if xp == VMA_NIL
                break
            if x == vma_pool[xp].left
                var w = vma_pool[xp].right
                if w == VMA_NIL
                    break
                if vma_pool[w].color == VMA_RB_RED
                    vma_pool[w].color = VMA_RB_BLACK
                    vma_pool[xp].color = VMA_RB_RED
                    vma_rotate_left(t, xp)
                    w = vma_pool[xp].right
                    if w == VMA_NIL
                        break
                if vma_color(vma_pool[w].left) == VMA_RB_BLACK
                    if vma_color(vma_pool[w].right) == VMA_RB_BLACK
                        vma_pool[w].color = VMA_RB_RED
                        x = xp
                        xp = vma_pool[x].parent
                        continue
                if vma_color(vma_pool[w].right) == VMA_RB_BLACK
                    vma_set_color(vma_pool[w].left, VMA_RB_BLACK)
                    vma_pool[w].color = VMA_RB_RED
                    vma_rotate_right(t, w)
                    w = vma_pool[xp].right
                vma_pool[w].color = vma_pool[xp].color
                vma_pool[xp].color = VMA_RB_BLACK
                vma_set_color(vma_pool[w].right, VMA_RB_BLACK)
                vma_rotate_left(t, xp)
                x = t.root
                break
            else
                var w = vma_pool[xp].left
                if w == VMA_NIL
                    break
                if vma_pool[w].color == VMA_RB_RED
                    vma_pool[w].color = VMA_RB_BLACK
                    vma_pool[xp].color = VMA_RB_RED
                    vma_rotate_right(t, xp)
                    w = vma_pool[xp].left
                    if w == VMA_NIL
                        break
                if vma_color(vma_pool[w].right) == VMA_RB_BLACK
                    if vma_color(vma_pool[w].left) == VMA_RB_BLACK
                        vma_pool[w].color = VMA_RB_RED
                        x = xp
                        xp = vma_pool[x].parent
                        continue
                if vma_color(vma_pool[w].left) == VMA_RB_BLACK
                    vma_set_color(vma_pool[w].right, VMA_RB_BLACK)
                    vma_pool[w].color = VMA_RB_RED
                    vma_rotate_left(t, w)
                    w = vma_pool[xp].left
                vma_pool[w].color = vma_pool[xp].color
                vma_pool[xp].color = VMA_RB_BLACK
                vma_set_color(vma_pool[w].left, VMA_RB_BLACK)
                vma_rotate_right(t, xp)
                x = t.root
                break
        vma_set_color(x, VMA_RB_BLACK)

Detach a VMA from the tree and return its slot to the pool. The
node index is invalidated after this call.

    vma_remove(t: *VMATree, z: int)
        if z == VMA_NIL
            return
        val y_orig_color = vma_pool[z].color
        var x = VMA_NIL
        var xp = VMA_NIL
        if vma_pool[z].left == VMA_NIL
            x = vma_pool[z].right
            xp = vma_pool[z].parent
            vma_transplant(t, z, vma_pool[z].right)
        else
            if vma_pool[z].right == VMA_NIL
                x = vma_pool[z].left
                xp = vma_pool[z].parent
                vma_transplant(t, z, vma_pool[z].left)
            else
                var y = vma_pool[z].right
                while vma_pool[y].left != VMA_NIL
                    y = vma_pool[y].left
                val y_color = vma_pool[y].color
                x = vma_pool[y].right
                if vma_pool[y].parent == z
                    xp = y
                else
                    xp = vma_pool[y].parent
                    vma_transplant(t, y, vma_pool[y].right)
                    vma_pool[y].right = vma_pool[z].right
                    vma_pool[vma_pool[y].right].parent = y
                vma_transplant(t, z, y)
                vma_pool[y].left = vma_pool[z].left
                vma_pool[vma_pool[y].left].parent = y
                vma_pool[y].color = vma_pool[z].color
                if y_color == VMA_RB_BLACK
                    vma_remove_fixup(t, x, xp)
                vma_free(z)
                return
        if y_orig_color == VMA_RB_BLACK
            vma_remove_fixup(t, x, xp)
        vma_free(z)

Split a VMA at `addr` (which must satisfy `start < addr < end`).
The original VMA is shrunk to `[start, addr)`; a new VMA covering
`[addr, end)` with the same prot/flags/fd is allocated and
inserted. Returns the new (right-half) VMA's index, or VMA_NIL on
allocator failure or invalid `addr`.

For file-backed VMAs, the right half's `offset` advances by
`(addr - original_start)` so file-byte alignment is preserved.

    vma_split(t: *VMATree, idx: int, addr: u64) -> int
        if idx == VMA_NIL
            return VMA_NIL
        if addr <= vma_pool[idx].vstart
            return VMA_NIL
        if addr >= vma_pool[idx].vend
            return VMA_NIL
        val new_idx = vma_alloc()
        if new_idx == VMA_NIL
            return VMA_NIL
        val orig_start = vma_pool[idx].vstart
        val orig_end   = vma_pool[idx].vend
        vma_pool[new_idx].vstart  = addr
        vma_pool[new_idx].vend    = orig_end
        vma_pool[new_idx].prot   = vma_pool[idx].prot
        vma_pool[new_idx].flags  = vma_pool[idx].flags
        vma_pool[new_idx].fd     = vma_pool[idx].fd
        vma_pool[new_idx].offset = vma_pool[idx].offset + (addr - orig_start)
        vma_pool[idx].vend = addr
        vma_insert(t, new_idx)
        new_idx

Tear down every VMA in a process. Used by `reap_process` and by
the future `execve` path. After the call, the tree is empty and
every node is back on the free list.

We can't walk the tree while remove-rebalancing it, so we collect
indices into a small stack via repeated `vma_first` calls. Pool
size bounds the total work.

    vma_clear_all(t: *VMATree)
        var n = vma_first(t)
        while n != VMA_NIL
            vma_remove(t, n)
            n = vma_first(t)

Counts. Used by tests; cheap to compute via in-order walk.

    vma_count(t: *VMATree) -> int
        var c = 0
        var n = vma_first(t)
        while n != VMA_NIL
            c += 1
            n = vma_next(n)
        c

Page-fault entry point. The arch handlers (x86 page-fault ISR,
aarch64 EL0 sync) call this with the faulting `vaddr`, the
process's `pid` and `ptbr`, and a `write` flag (1 if the fault
was caused by a store, 0 if a load or instruction fetch).

Returns 0 if the fault was satisfied (a frame was installed and
the faulting instruction can retry), -1 if no VMA covers the
address or the fault couldn't be handled here. The arch code
falls through to its legacy "kill process" path on -1.

Two distinct dispositions, separated by whether the leaf PTE is
already present:

  - **PTE absent** — first touch of an anonymous page. Allocate a
    zero frame via `kalloc_user_page` (refcount=1) and install it.
    Read/exec/write all funnel through this path on first access.

  - **PTE present** (write fault only — RO reads don't trap on
    either arch) — copy-on-write. The frame is shared (refcount
    set by some prior path: chunk-6 fork, the chunk-5 selftest's
    `sys_cow_share_self`, or a future shared-anon mapping). We
    allocate a fresh frame, copy the old contents into it, install
    the new frame writable, then drop our reference to the old
    frame. If we held the last ref the old frame returns to the
    page pool; otherwise it stays mapped wherever else it lives.

Shared mappings (MAP_SHARED) write-faulting on a RO PTE would
propagate the change to all sharers; the chunk-5 path doesn't yet
implement that, so we decline. MAP_PRIVATE is the only flag that
exists in chunk-5 user-space anyway.

    vma_handle_fault(t: *VMATree, vaddr: u64, ptbr: u64, write: int) -> int
        val v = vma_lookup_addr(t, vaddr)
        if v == VMA_NIL
            return -1
        if (vma_pool[v].flags & VMA_MAP_ANON) == 0
            return -1
        if write != 0
            if (vma_pool[v].prot & VMA_PROT_WRITE) == 0
                return -1
        else
            if (vma_pool[v].prot & VMA_PROT_READ) == 0
                return -1

        val page_va = vaddr & ~u64(0xFFF)
        var writable = 0
        if (vma_pool[v].prot & VMA_PROT_WRITE) != 0
            writable = 1
        var executable = 0
        if (vma_pool[v].prot & VMA_PROT_EXEC) != 0
            executable = 1

        val existing_pa = vm_v2p(ptbr, page_va)
        if existing_pa != u64(0)
            // PTE present — only reachable through a write fault on a
            // RO PTE. Defensive: a read fault on a present page should
            // never trap, but if it does, decline rather than silently
            // re-zero the page.
            if write == 0
                return -1
            if (vma_pool[v].flags & VMA_MAP_PRIVATE) == 0
                return -1
            val new_pa = kalloc_user_page()
            if new_pa == u64(0)
                return -1
            // Copy old frame → new frame. Both PAs sit in the page
            // pool, which both arches identity-map in kernel context
            // (x86 boot.s pd[0..511] superpages, aarch64 L1[1] block),
            // so PA = VA when reading from kernel mode.
            var src: *byte = *byte(existing_pa)
            var dst: *byte = *byte(new_pa)
            var i = 0
            while i < PAGE_SIZE
                dst[i] = src[i]
                i += 1
            if vm_install_user_page(ptbr, page_va, new_pa, writable, executable) != 0
                kfree_user_page(new_pa)
                return -1
            kfree_user_page(existing_pa)
            return 0

PTE absent — first-touch anonymous demand-page. `kalloc_user_page`
returns a zeroed frame at refcount=1.

        val pa = kalloc_user_page()
        if pa == u64(0)
            return -1
        if vm_install_user_page(ptbr, page_va, pa, writable, executable) != 0
            kfree_user_page(pa)
            return -1
        0


Clone a parent's VMA tree and resident pages into a freshly-
created child for `fork()` (chunk 6). For every parent VMA we
allocate a matching child VMA with the same range, prot, flags,
fd and offset; for every page that's currently resident in the
parent's PT we share the frame into the child's PT and demote
both PTEs to read-only so the next write traps into
`vma_handle_fault`'s COW branch.

The protocol matches the chunk-5 `sys_cow_share_self` shape and
relies on the same identity-map invariant: kernel mode sees PA =
VA in the page-pool window on both arches, so a single
`vm_install_user_page` into `child_ptbr` followed by a
`vm_update_user_prot` on `parent_ptbr` is enough — no cross-AS
copy is needed for the page contents because the frame is
genuinely shared until somebody writes.

  * **Refcount accounting.** `page_refcnt_share` migrates frames
    that were never refcount-tracked (raw `vm_copy_to` allocate-
    on-write under chunk-3) into the domain at fork time, so a
    later `kfree_user_page` from either side correctly
    decrements. Frames already tracked (chunk-5 demand-page
    fault path) just see refcount += 1.
  * **Read-only promotion.** Pages from a writable VMA get their
    parent PTE flipped to RO; pages from a read-only VMA already
    were RO and we only need to install the same RO mapping in
    the child. Either way, the *child's* mapping is installed RO
    here — when the child later writes, COW allocates it a fresh
    frame.

Returns 0 on full success. On any failure (VMA pool exhaustion,
`vm_install_user_page` rejection, `vm_update_user_prot`
rejection) returns -1; the caller (`kernel_fork`) is responsible
for tearing down the partially-populated child via the same
`kernel_reap_user_pages` + `vm_free_process_pt` path used on
process exit, which correctly drops the bumped refcounts and
releases the child's PT pages. We don't try to roll back the
parent-side RO demotions inline — they're harmless (a subsequent
write hits COW just like any other shared frame would) and
rolling back would require an extra walk of the PT.

    vma_clone_for_fork(parent_t: *VMATree, child_t: *VMATree,
                       parent_ptbr: u64, child_ptbr: u64) -> int
        var n = vma_first(parent_t)
        while n != VMA_NIL
            val pv_start  = vma_pool[n].vstart
            val pv_end    = vma_pool[n].vend
            val pv_prot   = vma_pool[n].prot
            val pv_flags  = vma_pool[n].flags
            val pv_fd     = vma_pool[n].fd
            val pv_offset = vma_pool[n].offset

            if vma_create(child_t, pv_start, pv_end, pv_prot,
                          pv_flags, pv_fd, pv_offset) == VMA_NIL
                return -1

PROT_NONE VMAs (e.g. the cross-arch guard region at
`[0x5FFFF000, 0x60000000)`) have no leaf pages to share — they
exist only to make any access fault into `vma_handle_fault` and
get rejected. `vm_v2p` on those VAs may return non-zero because
the kernel-identity 2 MiB superpage covers them at the PD level,
but `vm_install_user_page` would refuse anyway (the PD slot is
a block descriptor, not a table). Skip the per-page walk for
prot==0 VMAs — the cloned VMA struct is enough.

            if pv_prot != VMA_PROT_NONE
                var executable = 0
                if (pv_prot & VMA_PROT_EXEC) != 0
                    executable = 1
                var parent_writable = 0
                if (pv_prot & VMA_PROT_WRITE) != 0
                    parent_writable = 1

                var va = pv_start
                while va < pv_end
                    val ppa = vm_v2p(parent_ptbr, va)
                    if ppa != u64(0)
                        page_refcnt_share(ppa)
                        if vm_install_user_page(child_ptbr, va, ppa, 0, executable) != 0
                            kfree_user_page(ppa)
                            return -1
                        if parent_writable != 0
                            if vm_update_user_prot(parent_ptbr, va, 0, executable) != 0
                                return -1
                    va += u64(PAGE_SIZE)

            n = vma_next(n)
        0


Walk every VMA in `t` and release the page (if any) backing each
covered VA in `ptbr`. Used by `reap_process` (chunk 6) to drop the
process's references to its user pages before the page-table
structures themselves are freed by `vm_free_process_pt`. After
this call every leaf PTE under a VMA is cleared and any refcount
the process held on a frame has been dropped via
`kfree_user_page` (which page_free's the frame at refcount=0).

`vm_unmap_user_page` returns the cleared PA so we don't have to
walk the PT twice; a non-mapped slot returns 0 and we skip.
`kfree_user_page` correctly handles both refcount-tracked frames
(decrement; release at zero) and untracked frames (release
immediately) per chunk-6's `page_refcnt.lsysl` extension, so this
loop is correct regardless of how the leaf got into the PT.

    vma_unmap_all_pages(t: *VMATree, ptbr: u64)
        var n = vma_first(t)
        while n != VMA_NIL
            var va = vma_pool[n].vstart
            val vend = vma_pool[n].vend
            while va < vend
                val pa = vm_unmap_user_page(ptbr, va)
                if pa != u64(0)
                    kfree_user_page(pa)
                va += u64(PAGE_SIZE)
            n = vma_next(n)


Default user-space layout — single source of truth
--------------------------------------------------

The four constants below describe the layout that
`vma_seed_default_layout` (kernel.lsysl) installs in every new
process. They live here rather than in `oskit/arch/*/prog_config.sysl`
so that the VMA module owns the policy and downstream callers
(PM, RS, hello.lsysl) read it through `vma_default_initial_sp` /
`vma_default_stack_low` instead of arch-named PROG_USP / PROG_SSP
constants. The values are unchanged from the bring-up shape; only
the source-of-truth moves.

Phase B of the Phase 1 invariant-#1 rollout deletes the
`prog_config.sysl` duplicates and points consumers (POSIX
`brk` ceiling, etc.) at these instead.

    const VMA_DEFAULT_CODE_BOT  = 0x60000000
    const VMA_DEFAULT_CODE_TOP  = 0x60080000
    const VMA_DEFAULT_STACK_BOT = 0x60080000
    const VMA_DEFAULT_STACK_TOP = 0x60090000

The brk-arena bounds. Matches the `POSIX_BRK_BASE` / `POSIX_BRK_LIMIT`
that `oskit.posix.shim` uses to bookkeep `sys_brk`, so the
brk pointer is always inside this VMA. The seeded default
layout covers the same span as part of its single big code VMA;
the VMA-based PIE loader (`pm_load_image_pie`) installs this as
a separate anonymous RW VMA so musl's first malloc-via-brk
demand-faults a page in instead of crashing on an unmapped
heap.

    const VMA_DEFAULT_HEAP_BOT  = 0x60040000
    const VMA_DEFAULT_HEAP_TOP  = 0x60080000

`VMA_USER_VA_BOT` / `VMA_USER_VA_TOP` describe the full user
virtual-address window — the range every per-process user mapping
(code, stack, heap, mmap, ELF interp) lives inside. The kernel
helper `kernel_vm_copy_to` uses these as the strict-VMA-authority
band; `sys_mmap` uses them as the search window for
`vma_find_free_range`. Both arches' `vm_create_process_pt` build
**8 × 2 MiB = 16 MiB** of 4 KB-page leaf coverage for this window
(aarch64: 8 L3 tables under L2[256..263]; x86: 8 PTs under
pd1[256..263]). If the window ever grows further, bump
`VMA_USER_VA_TOP` *and* extend the leaf-table count in both
`vm_create_process_pt` / `vm_free_process_pt` in lockstep —
allocating a VMA above the leaf coverage would fault on first
touch with no leaf table to install into.

    const VMA_USER_VA_BOT = 0x60000000
    const VMA_USER_VA_TOP = 0x61000000

`vma_default_initial_sp` / `vma_default_stack_low` are the
arg-less queries PM and RS use to compute a child's initial SP
*before* the child's VMA tree exists — the seed runs inside
`create_process_suspended`, so there is no per-process tree to
walk at the moment the caller picks an SP. The values match what
`vma_seed_default_layout` would have written for that process, so
PM/RS computing the SP from these constants and the kernel
seeding the stack VMA agree by construction.

    vma_default_initial_sp() -> u64
        u64(VMA_DEFAULT_STACK_TOP)

    vma_default_stack_low() -> u64
        u64(VMA_DEFAULT_STACK_BOT)

`vma_initial_sp` / `vma_stack_low` walk an existing process's VMA
tree to find the canonical stack region — the highest-vend
anonymous VMA whose protection is exactly `READ|WRITE` (the seed
function writes the stack VMA RW with no EXEC bit; the code VMA
is RWX). Returns 0 if no stack VMA is found. Used by future
callers that need to read a child's stack range *after* the
child exists (e.g. signal delivery picking an SP).

    vma_initial_sp(t: *VMATree) -> u64
        val rw = VMA_PROT_READ | VMA_PROT_WRITE
        var best: u64 = u64(0)
        var n = vma_first(t)
        while n != VMA_NIL
            if vma_pool[n].prot == rw
                if (vma_pool[n].flags & VMA_MAP_ANON) != 0
                    if vma_pool[n].vend > best
                        best = vma_pool[n].vend
            n = vma_next(n)
        best

    vma_stack_low(t: *VMATree) -> u64
        val rw = VMA_PROT_READ | VMA_PROT_WRITE
        var best_end: u64 = u64(0)
        var best_start: u64 = u64(0)
        var n = vma_first(t)
        while n != VMA_NIL
            if vma_pool[n].prot == rw
                if (vma_pool[n].flags & VMA_MAP_ANON) != 0
                    if vma_pool[n].vend > best_end
                        best_end = vma_pool[n].vend
                        best_start = vma_pool[n].vstart
            n = vma_next(n)
        best_start


Address allocation helpers
--------------------------

`vma_find_free_range` walks the tree in order and returns the
lowest aligned vaddr `>= low` where a `len`-byte run fits without
overlapping any existing VMA, capped at `high`. Returns `u64(0)`
on no-fit. This is the policy `sys_mmap` uses now that the
old POSIX_MMAP_BASE/LIMIT bump-pointer arena is gone — the VMA
tree is the sole authority over user-space layout.

Bottom-up first-fit. Linux's `arch_get_unmapped_area_topdown` is
top-down, which avoids growing the heap and the mmap arena into
each other on long-running processes; with our small per-process
window and short-lived test processes, bottom-up is simpler and
the difference is academic.

    vma_find_free_range(t: *VMATree, len: u64, align: u64, low: u64, high: u64) -> u64
        if len == u64(0)
            return u64(0)
        if align == u64(0)
            return u64(0)
        if low >= high
            return u64(0)
        val mask = align - u64(1)
        var cur = (low + mask) & ~mask
        var n = vma_first(t)
        while n != VMA_NIL
            val vstart = vma_pool[n].vstart
            val vend   = vma_pool[n].vend
            if vend <= cur
                n = vma_next(n)
                continue
            if vstart >= cur + len
                if cur + len > high
                    return u64(0)
                return cur
            val after = (vend + mask) & ~mask
            if after > cur
                cur = after
            n = vma_next(n)
        if cur + len > high
            return u64(0)
        cur


`vma_range_free` answers "is `[start, end)` entirely free in the
tree?". O(log n) — point-lookup at `start`, then a forward walk
until we either pass `end` (free) or trip over a VMA whose start
falls inside the range (overlap). Used by `MAP_FIXED` to reject
overlap, matching Linux's "MAP_FIXED requires the range to be
unmapped or the call fails" semantics that musl tolerates here
(we never auto-replace, the way `MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE` would).

    vma_range_free(t: *VMATree, range_start: u64, range_end: u64) -> int
        if range_end <= range_start
            return 0
        if vma_lookup_addr(t, range_start) != VMA_NIL
            return 0
        var n = vma_first(t)
        while n != VMA_NIL
            val vstart = vma_pool[n].vstart
            if vstart >= range_end
                return 1
            if vstart >= range_start
                return 0
            n = vma_next(n)
        1


Selftest -- exercises the public surface and validates invariants
without going through any kernel state. Used by the chunk-1
test program via a debug syscall.

Returns 0 on success; 1..N on failure at step N.

    vma_selftest() -> int
        var t: VMATree
        vma_tree_init(&t)

        // Step 1 — insert three disjoint VMAs in non-sorted order.
        val a = vma_create(&t, u64(0x10000), u64(0x20000), VMA_PROT_READ, VMA_MAP_ANON | VMA_MAP_PRIVATE, -1, u64(0))
        if a == VMA_NIL
            return 1
        val b = vma_create(&t, u64(0x40000), u64(0x50000), VMA_PROT_READ | VMA_PROT_WRITE, VMA_MAP_ANON | VMA_MAP_PRIVATE, -1, u64(0))
        if b == VMA_NIL
            return 1
        val c = vma_create(&t, u64(0x30000), u64(0x35000), VMA_PROT_READ, VMA_MAP_ANON | VMA_MAP_PRIVATE, -1, u64(0))
        if c == VMA_NIL
            return 1
        if vma_count(&t) != 3
            return 2

        // Step 3 — point lookups inside, at boundaries, between.
        if vma_lookup_addr(&t, u64(0x10000)) != a
            return 3
        if vma_lookup_addr(&t, u64(0x1FFFF)) != a
            return 3
        if vma_lookup_addr(&t, u64(0x20000)) != VMA_NIL
            return 3
        if vma_lookup_addr(&t, u64(0x32000)) != c
            return 3
        if vma_lookup_addr(&t, u64(0x45678)) != b
            return 3
        if vma_lookup_addr(&t, u64(0x60000)) != VMA_NIL
            return 3

        // Step 4 — split b at midpoint; verify both halves still resolve.
        val b_right = vma_split(&t, b, u64(0x48000))
        if b_right == VMA_NIL
            return 4
        if vma_count(&t) != 4
            return 4
        if vma_lookup_addr(&t, u64(0x47000)) != b
            return 4
        if vma_lookup_addr(&t, u64(0x49000)) != b_right
            return 4
        if vma_pool[b].vend != u64(0x48000)
            return 4
        if vma_pool[b_right].vstart != u64(0x48000)
            return 4
        if vma_pool[b_right].vend != u64(0x50000)
            return 4

        // Step 5 — bad split args reject without leaking a slot.
        val pre_count = vma_count(&t)
        if vma_split(&t, a, u64(0x10000)) != VMA_NIL
            return 5
        if vma_split(&t, a, u64(0x20000)) != VMA_NIL
            return 5
        if vma_split(&t, a, u64(0x80000)) != VMA_NIL
            return 5
        if vma_count(&t) != pre_count
            return 5

        // Step 6 — remove the right-half of b and verify the lookup
        // for that range now misses.
        vma_remove(&t, b_right)
        if vma_count(&t) != 3
            return 6
        if vma_lookup_addr(&t, u64(0x49000)) != VMA_NIL
            return 6
        if vma_lookup_addr(&t, u64(0x47000)) != b
            return 6

        // Step 7 — vma_first / vma_next yield VMAs in ascending order.
        var prev_start: u64 = 0
        var n = vma_first(&t)
        while n != VMA_NIL
            if vma_pool[n].vstart < prev_start
                return 7
            prev_start = vma_pool[n].vstart
            n = vma_next(n)

        // Step 8 — clear_all returns every slot to the pool.
        vma_clear_all(&t)
        if vma_count(&t) != 0
            return 8
        if t.root != VMA_NIL
            return 8

        // Step 9 — large fan-out exercise that forces rebalancing.
        // Insert 64 VMAs with strides of 2 pages, then pick three at
        // random and remove them, then walk in order and verify
        // count + monotonicity.
        var i = 0
        while i < 64
            val s = u64(0x100000) + u64(i) * u64(0x2000)
            val e = s + u64(0x1000)
            if vma_create(&t, s, e, VMA_PROT_READ, VMA_MAP_ANON | VMA_MAP_PRIVATE, -1, u64(0)) == VMA_NIL
                return 9
            i += 1
        if vma_count(&t) != 64
            return 9
        // Remove the 0th, 32nd, and 63rd by point-lookup.
        val r0 = vma_lookup_addr(&t, u64(0x100000))
        val r1 = vma_lookup_addr(&t, u64(0x100000) + u64(32) * u64(0x2000))
        val r2 = vma_lookup_addr(&t, u64(0x100000) + u64(63) * u64(0x2000))
        if r0 == VMA_NIL
            return 9
        if r1 == VMA_NIL
            return 9
        if r2 == VMA_NIL
            return 9
        vma_remove(&t, r0)
        vma_remove(&t, r1)
        vma_remove(&t, r2)
        if vma_count(&t) != 61
            return 9
        // Walk + monotonicity check.
        var prev: u64 = 0
        var n2 = vma_first(&t)
        while n2 != VMA_NIL
            if vma_pool[n2].vstart < prev
                return 9
            prev = vma_pool[n2].vstart
            n2 = vma_next(n2)
        vma_clear_all(&t)

        // Step 10 — default-layout helpers return the same values the
        // seed function would have written into the stack VMA.
        if vma_default_initial_sp() != u64(VMA_DEFAULT_STACK_TOP)
            return 10
        if vma_default_stack_low() != u64(VMA_DEFAULT_STACK_BOT)
            return 10

        // Step 11 — vma_initial_sp / vma_stack_low pick the highest-vend
        // RW anon VMA out of a mixed-prot tree. Set up: one RWX code
        // VMA (mimicking the seed's code carve-out) plus two RW anon
        // VMAs at different addresses; the higher-vend one wins.
        val rwx_step11 = VMA_PROT_READ | VMA_PROT_WRITE | VMA_PROT_EXEC
        val rw_step11  = VMA_PROT_READ | VMA_PROT_WRITE
        val anon_priv  = VMA_MAP_ANON | VMA_MAP_PRIVATE
        if vma_create(&t, u64(0x60000000), u64(0x60080000), rwx_step11, anon_priv, -1, u64(0)) == VMA_NIL
            return 11
        if vma_create(&t, u64(0x60080000), u64(0x60090000), rw_step11, anon_priv, -1, u64(0)) == VMA_NIL
            return 11
        if vma_create(&t, u64(0x70000000), u64(0x70001000), rw_step11, anon_priv, -1, u64(0)) == VMA_NIL
            return 11
        if vma_initial_sp(&t) != u64(0x70001000)
            return 11
        if vma_stack_low(&t) != u64(0x70000000)
            return 11
        vma_clear_all(&t)

        // Step 12 — empty tree returns 0 from both helpers (no stack VMA).
        if vma_initial_sp(&t) != u64(0)
            return 12
        if vma_stack_low(&t) != u64(0)
            return 12

        // Step 13 — vma_find_free_range first-fit. Lay out two VMAs
        // leaving a 0x10000 gap between them, then ask for 0x4000;
        // expect the lowest address inside the gap.
        val rw_step13 = VMA_PROT_READ | VMA_PROT_WRITE
        val anon_step13 = VMA_MAP_ANON | VMA_MAP_PRIVATE
        if vma_create(&t, u64(0x60000000), u64(0x60010000), rw_step13, anon_step13, -1, u64(0)) == VMA_NIL
            return 13
        if vma_create(&t, u64(0x60020000), u64(0x60030000), rw_step13, anon_step13, -1, u64(0)) == VMA_NIL
            return 13
        val fit = vma_find_free_range(&t, u64(0x4000), u64(0x1000), u64(0x60000000), u64(0x60200000))
        if fit != u64(0x60010000)
            return 13
        // A request that doesn't fit in any gap walks past the last VMA
        // and lands at the post-last position (aligned).
        val fit2 = vma_find_free_range(&t, u64(0x100000), u64(0x1000), u64(0x60000000), u64(0x60200000))
        if fit2 != u64(0x60030000)
            return 13
        // A request that exceeds `high` returns 0.
        val nope = vma_find_free_range(&t, u64(0x200000), u64(0x1000), u64(0x60000000), u64(0x60200000))
        if nope != u64(0)
            return 13
        // Alignment: 1 MiB align skips past the existing VMA to 0x60100000.
        val aligned = vma_find_free_range(&t, u64(0x4000), u64(0x100000), u64(0x60000000), u64(0x60200000))
        if aligned != u64(0x60100000)
            return 13

        // Step 14 — vma_range_free recognises free and occupied
        // ranges in the same tree.
        if vma_range_free(&t, u64(0x60010000), u64(0x60020000)) != 1
            return 14
        if vma_range_free(&t, u64(0x60005000), u64(0x60008000)) != 0
            return 14
        if vma_range_free(&t, u64(0x6000F000), u64(0x60011000)) != 0
            return 14
        if vma_range_free(&t, u64(0x60100000), u64(0x60110000)) != 1
            return 14
        vma_clear_all(&t)

        // Step 15 — empty tree: the entire [low, high) window is free.
        if vma_find_free_range(&t, u64(0x10000), u64(0x1000), u64(0x60000000), u64(0x60200000)) != u64(0x60000000)
            return 15
        if vma_range_free(&t, u64(0x60000000), u64(0x60200000)) != 1
            return 15

        // Step 16 — large fan-out beyond the legacy 256-slot cap. Inserts
        // 500 disjoint 1-page VMAs with a 2-page stride, walks them in
        // ascending order, and clears. Fails if the pool can't grow past
        // 256 (regression-pin for the MAX_VMAS bump).
        var j = 0
        while j < 500
            val s = u64(0x40000000) + u64(j) * u64(0x2000)
            val e = s + u64(0x1000)
            if vma_create(&t, s, e, VMA_PROT_READ, VMA_MAP_ANON | VMA_MAP_PRIVATE, -1, u64(0)) == VMA_NIL
                return 16
            j += 1
        if vma_count(&t) != 500
            return 16
        var prev3: u64 = 0
        var n3 = vma_first(&t)
        while n3 != VMA_NIL
            if vma_pool[n3].vstart < prev3
                return 16
            prev3 = vma_pool[n3].vstart
            n3 = vma_next(n3)
        vma_clear_all(&t)
        if vma_count(&t) != 0
            return 16

        0
